==German==

===Etymology===
From {{der|de|en|Wayne}}, which sounds somewhat similar to German {{m|de|wen}} (“whom”, pronounced {{IPAchar|[veːn]}} or {{IPAchar|[ʋeːn]}}). The sense is derived from trick questions of the sort: „''Kennst du Wayne?''“ – ? – „''Wayne interessiert’s.''“, that is: {{m|de|wen interessiert's}}. (“Do you know Wayne?” – ? – “Wayne cares.” [= “Who cares.”])

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/wɛɪ̯n/|/weːn/}}

===Interjection===
{{head|de|interjection}}

# {{lb|de|youth|_|slang}} [[who cares]]
#: {{ux|de|Helmut Schmidt ist tot. — Ach, '''wayne'''.|[[w:Helmut Schmidt|Helmut Schmidt]] is dead. — Ah, '''who cares'''.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|de|mir doch egal}}
* {{l|de|wen interessiert's}}

